{"text": "Begin before any print on this wall. At the Heian court by the mid-12th century, the kosode was underwear worn next to the skin. As court dress grew simpler, the robes above it came off and the kosode came to the surface. By the early Edo period, its shape was not far from today's kimono. A pattern book printed in 1666 shows the large flowing designs of the Kanbun style, a wide empty back with large moving motifs. Meiji changed the words. Many garment names collapsed into one, kimono. Becca argues that one shape was frozen in time in these years. In one kimono magazine, the tsukesage had nearly vanished by the 2000s, folded into the visiting kimono. In 1981, the kimono market was worth nearly 1.8 trillion yen. By 2020, it was about a seventh of that. Kimono came to be seen as something rented rather than owned. By 2021, Kyoto alone had about 40 rental shops. By then, the shape had long since traveled. The wall goes on abroad.", "duration": 72.8, "timestamps": {"word": [{"word": "Begin", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.64}, {"word": "before", "start": 1.02, "end": 1.36}, {"word": "any", "start": 1.46, "end": 1.66}, {"word": "print", "start": 1.8, "end": 2.1}, {"word": "on", "start": 2.14, "end": 2.24}, {"word": "this", "start": 2.28, "end": 2.48}, {"word": "wall.", "start": 2.56, "end": 2.86}, {"word": "At", "start": 3.52, "end": 3.62}, {"word": "the", "start": 3.66, "end": 3.74}, {"word": "Heian", "start": 3.8, "end": 4.18}, {"word": "court", "start": 4.3, "end": 4.66}, {"word": "by", "start": 4.72, "end": 4.82}, {"word": "the", "start": 4.86, "end": 4.92}, {"word": "mid-12th", "start": 4.98, "end": 5.46}, {"word": "century,", "start": 5.54, "end": 5.98}, {"word": "the", "start": 6.14, "end": 6.24}, {"word": "kosode", "start": 6.3, "end": 6.7}, {"word": "was", "start": 7.18, "end": 7.36}, {"word": "underwear", "start": 7.44, "end": 7.98}, {"word": "worn", "start": 8.38, "end": 8.58}, {"word": "next", "start": 8.64, "end": 8.86}, {"word": "to", "start": 8.92, "end":